<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Lesotho</h1><p class="st-meta">1966 CE – present · capital Maseru</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The independent mountain kingdom of the Basotho, entirely surrounded by South Africa, since 1966.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/basutoland">Basutoland</a> became independent on 4 October 1966 as the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/basotho-kingdom">Lesotho</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-nations">Commonwealth</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-ii">Moshoeshoe II</a>, a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-i">Moshoeshoe I</a> of the Basotho Kingdom, as king and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leabua-jonathan">Leabua Jonathan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basotho-national-party">Basotho National Party</a> as prime minister.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Jonathan annulled the 1970 election he was losing and ruled by decree until a military coup in 1986. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-ii">Moshoeshoe II</a> was exiled in 1990 and replaced by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/letsie-iii">Letsie III</a>, then briefly restored before his death in 1996. The economy relies on mine wages from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">South Africa</a>, textiles and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/basotho-kingdom">Lesotho</a> Highlands Water Project, which sells water to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauteng">Gauteng</a>.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>A disputed election in 1998 led to rioting and intervention by South African and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a> troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-african-development-community">SADC</a>. Since 2002 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/basotho-kingdom">Lesotho</a> has held regular elections under a mixed electoral system, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/letsie-iii">Letsie III</a> as king.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-lesotho.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Lesotho" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Lesotho.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1966 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Maseru</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Moshoeshoe II, Leabua Jonathan, Letsie III</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sesotho, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/lesotho">Lesotho</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lesotho</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1996&amp;focus=kingdom-of-lesotho" aria-label="Kingdom of Lesotho on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5396 3249 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Lesotho lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5716 3505L5716 3505"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5716 3505h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5716 3505h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5716 3505h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Maseru. <a href="/atlas?year=1996&amp;focus=kingdom-of-lesotho">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
